estion/id #uuid "bd91585f-60ab-5f83-868d-d63a69da0526" :question/math-academy-id "q-158122" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26b1ea4a-8431-5e9b-892f-bca7c95f80b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388976} {:answer/id #uuid "a5c9edc5-9c1a-5e2a-b1fe-0d7188e07e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388977} {:answer/id #uuid "a1d236a8-2a2f-599a-b81e-073c9d88e925" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388978} {:answer/id #uuid "e60312e7-b230-5a43-aa7d-233414826603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388979} {:answer/id #uuid "82820516-f5de-5854-9a60-bb231ec12fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5c9edc5-9c1a-5e2a-b1fe-0d7188e07e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388977} :answer-field/id #uuid "c310404e-e4a9-55ab-a9ab-4c596d9b583c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f3f0d39-46aa-5c30-a041-815de875f218" :question/math-academy-id "q-158129" :question/problem "Verify whether the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent, and find the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the vectors ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}},$ where\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we verify whether ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are linearly independent. So, we start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, the pivot columns correspond to the linearly independent vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ that form the basis of our span.\n\nNow, the orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the linearly independent columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{18}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9f55b2-ce37-507f-8cb6-7d4e30e6868a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Subspace"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce4106d4-3066-525e-9cc4-3cc62600cc77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366113} {:answer/id #uuid "30e731b8-e203-5d2a-b715-03aab570da25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366114} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ec102b-6380-5917-b3e8-96986912f2e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366115} {:answer/id #uuid "353ce806-e08a-585e-aedc-1f31c4ef4e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366116} {:answer/id #uuid "88d5a945-073b-5c18-9a22-28e2025b07d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366117}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88d5a945-073b-5c18-9a22-28e2025b07d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366117} :answer-field/id #uuid "974cc47c-a0cf-5ce6-8700-2a3f5c0bccd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366112}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efe681da-e463-5585-903f-22f3b4456bf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-39815" :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by ${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{12} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44b61f5d-9f26-5dfb-88a2-5bd1553444cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394513} {:answer/id #uuid "313cef30-0e40-5e19-a20c-d67946ddd914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394514} {:answer/id #uuid "fea696ab-d539-5029-98a9-afe7403faef2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394515} {:answer/id #uuid "2bdc7bae-9c94-54c4-9dd8-3c37a0fc36ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394516} {:answer/id #uuid "5f6ab00e-9bd5-5a22-b9a4-3b4ae167be95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f6ab00e-9bd5-5a22-b9a4-3b4ae167be95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394517} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4acddf6-0263-54d3-b64b-9a44d6523c1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0bf1f01-07c1-5bc9-90e8-13db8dd459d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83563" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, &  \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }1\\,\\, \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,-1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c07721dd-4b97-5d57-8de1-5098117e3071" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Orthogonal Projection of a Vector Onto a Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "073d74ff-585a-5211-ab42-9064581e88f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348509} {:answer/id #uuid "59fc5c2f-0341-50bd-bee7-ed31a5e540a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349275} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8587e9-698e-5aac-bff3-351d87f2a068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349573} {:answer/id #uuid "f8595241-4221-55b2-ab69-0bdadd3f1407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350624} {:answer/id #uuid "fec525da-79fa-5e81-8939-908fa7601311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "073d74ff-585a-5211-ab42-9064581e88f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348509} :answer-field/id #uuid "19097d36-8180-55c4-bb9a-01abbd6396f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "be7ed026-22ac-5b61-bb76-23314f742216" :question/math-academy-id "q-83574" :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}{v,w}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 14\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
